@using XgFilter_Lib.Filtering
@using XgFilter_Razor.Components.Internal
@implements IDisposable

@* ── FilterSurface.razor ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The one consumer-facing filter component: owns FilterPanel + the saved-
   filters mount of NamedEntriesPanel and the interaction wiring between them
   — load→stage, save/save-as→snapshot-or-refuse, delete, the saved-filters
   degrade notices, and the setup-change rule for the saved-filters context.

   It holds no filter state. The selection, what is applied, and what this
   boot's restoration found belong to the app-scoped FilterSetup
   (halheinrich/backgammon#374, SPEC-filtering.md §4), which outlives this
   component and every page that mounts it; the host reports its source to
   that owner and reads its gate there. So the host binds only what is the
   host's to say here:

   - an IDocumentStorage adapter for the saved-filters document (null = no
     context),
   - the host's CanPersist capability ruling and its host-specific
     disabled-reason wording.

   Storage refusals go to the host's registered sink, not through this
   component (IFilterStorageRefusalSink): a refusal can arrive after the
   component that started the call has gone.

   Notice copy lives here — the producer — so every host degrades with
   identical wording; only host-specific reasons (FS-Access phrasing etc.)
   arrive as parameters. No RenderFragment slots by verified YAGNI: neither
   host interleaves anything between this component's children.
   Location: XgFilter_Razor/Components/FilterSurface.razor
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@

@if (ShowSavedFiltersSection)
{
    <NamedEntriesPanel Document="_store.Document"
                       Surface="SavedFilters"
                       OnLoadRequested="HandleLoadRequested"
                       OnSaveRequested="HandleSaveRequested"
                       OnSaveAsRequested="HandleSaveRequested"
                       OnDeleteRequested="HandleDeleteRequested"
                       CanPersist="EffectiveCanPersist"
                       PersistDisabledReason="@EffectivePersistDisabledReason" />
}

@* Save refusal: the current filter holds a value that cannot be committed — an
   unparseable position pattern, an error bound the lib rules invalid, a box
   whose text its field cannot be — so the snapshot was refused (exactly the
   states Apply also refuses). The panel's confirm/name affordances have already
   reacted, so a silent no-op would read as a lost save — say why. The copy
   names no particular field: the offending one is already marked in the panel
   with its own explanation, and a second naming here would be a second
   encoding of which rule failed. Assertive, like a failed user action.

   An error, so dismissible (the umbrella's SPEC-notices.md, Fork A). The
   occurrence is one refused save and this component owns it outright:
   _saveRefusalNotice is non-null exactly while a refusal stands. So closing
   the box ends the occurrence — the field is cleared, as every other mooting
   gesture already clears it — and there is no dismissed bit anywhere, here or
   in the Notice (the binding makes it caller-held, so it keeps none). A
   second refusal assigns the field again and shows fresh although its text is
   identical: the text was never the occurrence. No @if around it, for the
   restore notice's reason — one fact, read once. *@
<Notice id="filterSaveError" class="mb-4" style="max-width:800px"
        Kind="NoticeKind.Error" Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Assertive"
        Dismissible="true"
        @bind-Dismissed:get="_saveRefusalNotice is null"
        @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => _saveRefusalNotice = null">
    @_saveRefusalNotice
</Notice>

@* Corrupt/unreadable saved-filters file: replaces the panel. The file is
   preserved untouched (never overwritten) and named exactly — canonical or
   legacy, whichever the load actually failed on. A polite outcome, not an
   error.

   A gate reason, so NOT dismissible (SPEC-notices.md, Fork C): it stands
   where the saved-filters panel would and is the only thing on screen saying
   why saving is off. It leaves when its cause does. *@
@if (_store.Status == NamedDocumentStatus.LoadFailed)
{
    <Notice id="savedFiltersLoadFailed" class="mb-4" style="max-width:800px"
            Kind="NoticeKind.Warning" Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Polite">
        Your saved-filters file (<strong>@_store.LoadFailedFileName</strong>)
        couldn't be read, so it's been left untouched — saving is disabled to
        protect it.
    </Notice>
}

@* Write-back failure: a save/delete couldn't be persisted. The in-memory list
   keeps the edit, but this component lives and dies with the page, so the
   copy promises page-lifetime retention only — never "kept for this session".
   Assertive: a save the user asked for didn't land.

   A condition notice, dismissible per failed write (SPEC-notices.md, Fork D).
   The status is the condition and not the occurrence — it reads WriteFailed
   after every failure alike — so the store, the only party that knows a write
   failed, names each one (LastWriteFailure) and that name is the occurrence
   key. The store and this notice both die with this component, so the Notice
   holds the bit itself; this component keeps no dismissed state and infers
   nothing from its own renders. *@
@if (_store.Status == NamedDocumentStatus.WriteFailed)
{
    <Notice id="savedFiltersWriteFailed" class="mb-4" style="max-width:800px"
            Kind="NoticeKind.Warning" Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Assertive"
            Dismissible="true" OccurrenceKey="_store.LastWriteFailure">
        Couldn't save to <strong>@SavedFiltersDocument.FileName</strong> — your
        change is kept in this list for now, but it is not being saved and will
        be lost when you leave this page or reload.
    </Notice>
}

<FilterPanel />

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The host's storage adapter for the saved-filters document, or
    /// <see langword="null"/> for a host (or mode) with no readable storage —
    /// then no saved-filters section renders at all. This component owns the
    /// <see cref="SavedFiltersStore"/> over it, rebuilt when the bound
    /// reference changes; because the store lives with this component, a
    /// remount re-reads the document (a setup-time, degrade-tolerant read).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IDocumentStorage? Storage { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The host's capability half of the persist gate — e.g. BgQuiz's
    /// writable-grant ruling. ANDed with the store's own
    /// <see cref="NamedDocumentStatus.Ready"/> before reaching the panel, so
    /// a degraded context disables saving regardless of capability.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool CanPersist { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>
    /// The host-specific wording shown while <see cref="CanPersist"/> is
    /// <see langword="false"/> (e.g. FS-Access "write access not granted"
    /// phrasing — the host knows why, this component doesn't). Forwarded only
    /// while the <em>host's</em> half disables persisting: a
    /// <see cref="NamedDocumentStatus.WriteFailed"/> disable is explained by
    /// its own louder notice instead.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? PersistDisabledReason { get; set; }

    // The app-scoped owner of the selection (SPEC-filtering.md §4). Injected:
    // the same instance the host reads its gate from, by construction.
    [Inject] private FilterSetup Setup { get; set; } = default!;

    // ── The saved-filters mount ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // The generic pick list knows no document, so the saved-filters identity
    // lives here, with the composite that mounts it. Every id and every phrase
    // below is pinned by suites in three repositories (this one, BgQuiz and
    // ExtractFromXgToCsv) — they are published surface, and a change here is a
    // change to those suites, not a rename.
    // Internal, not private: this repo's panel suite mounts the generic panel
    // with this very instance rather than retyping the ids and copy, which
    // would be the second encoding the preset exists to prevent. The same
    // producer-side idiom as FilterStorage's key constants; it widens
    // nothing for consumers.
    internal static readonly NamedEntriesSurface SavedFilters = new()
    {
        Title = "Saved Filters",
        EmptyText = "No saved filters yet.",
        NamePlaceholder = "Filter name",
        OverwriteWithNoun = "the current filters",
        NameInputId = "saveFilterName",
        SaveButtonId = "saveFilterButton",
        LoadedNoticeId = "savedFilterLoadedNotice",
    };

    // The composite-owned saved-filters lifecycle over the host's adapter.
    // Replaced (never mutated in place) when the Storage parameter changes;
    // starts over a null adapter so the pre-first-parameters state is the
    // same Disabled the null-adapter contract gives.
    private SavedFiltersStore _store = new(null);

    // The owner's latest snapshot, delivered on attach and replaced at every
    // change; and the attachment, released at disposal.
    private FilterSetupSnapshot _setup = default!;
    private IDisposable? _attachment;

    // Last-seen Storage parameter, for change detection; _initialized
    // separates the first parameters-set (build the store and load the
    // context — nothing else) from a genuine change.
    private bool _initialized;
    private IDocumentStorage? _lastStorage;

    // Composite-owned refusal copy for a save the panel optimistically
    // confirmed but the snapshot refused. Non-null exactly while a refusal
    // stands, which makes it the refusal notice's whole state: cleared on any
    // change to the selection or to what is applied, the next save attempt,
    // the end of a setup, and the user closing the notice.
    private string? _saveRefusalNotice;

    protected override void OnInitialized() => _attachment = Setup.Attach(OnSetupChanged);

    // ── The setup-change rule for the saved-filters context ─────────────────
    //
    // The first snapshot arrives inside Attach, before anything renders: it is
    // the existing setup, learned, and ends nothing — a remount over an
    // unchanged source keeps everything, which is what the owner is for. Every
    // later snapshot is a real change. A new generation is the end of a setup
    // (the host reported a different source): the refusal posed against the
    // outgoing context drops, and the context reloads through the seam — or
    // resets, when there is no source. A change to the selection or to what is
    // applied moots a stale save refusal, as any gesture on the panel always
    // has — fixing the offending value is one, and so is committing.
    private void OnSetupChanged(FilterSetupSnapshot next)
    {
        var previous = _setup;
        _setup = next;
        if (previous is null) return;

        if (next.Generation != previous.Generation)
        {
            _saveRefusalNotice = null;
            _ = ReloadAfterSetupEndedAsync();
            return;
        }

        if (!next.Draft.Equals(previous.Draft) || !Equals(next.Baseline, previous.Baseline))
        {
            _saveRefusalNotice = null;
        }

        _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
    }

    protected override async Task OnParametersSetAsync()
    {
        if (!_initialized)
        {
            _initialized = true;
            _lastStorage = Storage;
            _store = new SavedFiltersStore(Storage);
            await ReloadContextAsync();
            return;
        }

        if (!ReferenceEquals(_lastStorage, Storage))
        {
            _lastStorage = Storage;
            _store = new SavedFiltersStore(Storage);
            await ReloadContextAsync();
        }
    }

    // The reload a setup's end starts (halheinrich/backgammon#374, the
    // review's third correction). Nothing awaits it — the owner tells this
    // component inside its own operation, which cannot wait on a component's
    // I/O — so it is observed here. A document-storage failure never arrives:
    // the store catches it and degrades to LoadFailed, whose notice says so.
    // Anything else is a fault, an adapter's bug, and goes to the renderer's
    // error path as this component's (DispatchExceptionAsync): to an enclosing
    // ErrorBoundary, else to the host's unhandled-error handling. Discarded,
    // it would die on a task nobody reads and leave the previous source's
    // saved filters on screen.
    private async Task ReloadAfterSetupEndedAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            await InvokeAsync(ReloadContextAndRenderAsync);
        }
        catch (Exception failure)
        {
            await DispatchExceptionAsync(failure);
        }
    }

    private async Task ReloadContextAndRenderAsync()
    {
        await ReloadContextAsync();
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    private async Task ReloadContextAsync()
    {
        if (_setup.Source is null)
            _store.Reset();
        else
            await _store.LoadAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>Stops observing the owner; anything the owner is doing carries on without this component.</summary>
    public void Dispose() => _attachment?.Dispose();

    // ── Saved-filters mediation ─────────────────────────────────────────────

    // A miss is unreachable through the panel's own rows: every name the panel
    // can raise came from this very collection's Names. So a failed lookup is
    // the store and the panel having diverged — a bug here, not bad input —
    // and the silent no-op it used to be is the one path where the panel's
    // "{name} loaded." confirmation could outrun reality, standing over filters
    // that never moved (halheinrich/backgammon#173). Throwing is what keeps
    // that confirmation honest: the panel states it only once the request has
    // come back without throwing. A load is staged, never committed: an edit
    // like any other, the owner's.
    private void HandleLoadRequested(string name)
    {
        _saveRefusalNotice = null;
        if (!_store.Document.TryGet(name, out var config))
            throw new InvalidOperationException(
                $"The saved-filters document has no entry named '{name}'.");

        Setup.Stage(config);
    }

    // One handler for both save gestures — row Save and save-as differ only
    // in where the name comes from; the mediation (snapshot the live draft or
    // refuse) is identical by design. The snapshot is the draft's own verdict,
    // the one Apply reads for the values, so no saved document is minted from
    // a selection Apply would refuse for them.
    private async Task HandleSaveRequested(string name)
    {
        if (Setup.Current.TryGetSavable(out var config))
        {
            _saveRefusalNotice = null;
            await _store.SaveAsync(name, config);
        }
        else
        {
            _saveRefusalNotice =
                "The current filter has an invalid value, so it can't be saved "
                + "— fix it in the filters below, then save.";
        }
    }

    private async Task HandleDeleteRequested(string name)
    {
        _saveRefusalNotice = null;
        await _store.DeleteAsync(name);
    }

    // ── Saved-filters section visibility ────────────────────────────────────

    // Ready shows the panel unless the context is read-only AND empty —
    // nothing to load, nothing to save, so the section is pure clutter
    // (BgQuiz's ruling, now producer-owned). WriteFailed keeps the panel (the
    // in-memory list is still truthful) beside its notice; Disabled and
    // LoadFailed render no panel — the latter degrades to its notice.
    private bool ShowSavedFiltersSection => _store.Status switch
    {
        NamedDocumentStatus.Ready => CanPersist || _store.Document.Count > 0,
        NamedDocumentStatus.WriteFailed => true,
        _ => false,
    };

    private bool EffectiveCanPersist =>
        CanPersist && _store.Status == NamedDocumentStatus.Ready;

    private string? EffectivePersistDisabledReason =>
        !CanPersist ? PersistDisabledReason : null;
}
